{-
  A spin lock - the one form of mutual exclusion that is made of atomics and nothing else.

  `Atomic.native.yana` says that a mutex belongs above this module, and that is still true of the
  mutex a program will eventually want: one that stops burning the processor when it cannot make
  progress has to hand the processor back, and handing it back is a scheduler operation. This one
  never does. It is two atomic operations and a loop, so it has no dependency on anything the
  concurrency library will later supply, and it is here for that reason rather than as the beginning
  of a lock collection.

  § What it is for, and what it is not

  A spin lock is right when the critical section is **shorter than a context switch** and the holder
  cannot realistically be descheduled inside it: a few field updates, a free-list push, two words
  that have to move together. It is wrong for anything that allocates, takes another lock, calls
  into the operating system, or runs for an unbounded number of steps.

  The failure mode is not slowness, it is a cliff. A waiter burns a whole timeslice at full power
  while the holder is not running, and on a machine with more runnable waiters than cores that is the
  ordinary case rather than the unlucky one. So the rule a reader should take away is: **hold it for
  a bounded, tiny number of instructions, or use something else.** When the scheduler's parking
  operation exists, the something else will be a mutex that spins briefly and then parks - and it
  will be built out of `tryLock` and `unlock` below.

  § It is not reentrant

  Locking one this thread already holds deadlocks immediately, with no diagnostic: the loop simply
  never leaves. Detecting it means knowing *which* thread holds the lock, which means a thread
  identity to compare and a word to keep it in - a different type with a different cost. A recursive
  lock is also, usually, a design that has not decided who owns what.

  § Explicit first, scoped second

  `lock` and `unlock` are the primitive and are public, because a lock whose acquisition and release
  are in different functions is a real algorithm rather than a mistake - a hand-off, a state machine
  that acquires on entry to one state and releases on leaving another. `withLock` below is the form
  almost every caller should write instead, and it is a lens rather than a higher-order function, so
  the release runs on the early `return` out of the critical section too.

  A linear guard - a value that *is* the held lock and cannot be dropped without releasing it - is
  the third form and is not here. It wants a type the compiler refuses to discard, which is a
  language feature this module should consume rather than motivate; that and the real mutexes go
  together, later.

  § Native only, for `Atomic`'s reason

  There is no worker or shared-buffer model on the JavaScript target, so there is nothing for two
  holders of this to be.
-}
module Atomic

{-
  The lock word - §3.2's flag case, and one byte.

  `Atomic(Bool)` rather than `Atomic(U32)` because that is what the storage means: held or not, and
  nothing else. A payload-free enumeration in an atomic is exactly the family §3.2 admits, and `Bool`
  is one. A fair lock or one that counts waiters needs a word to keep a ticket or a count in, and
  that is a **different type** rather than this one widened - what differs is the operations, not the
  field.

  There is no padding to a cache line, which is a decision rather than an oversight. A lock sharing a
  line with the data it protects is the common case and usually the right one: what a waiter does
  immediately after acquiring is read that data, so it wanted the line anyway. What it costs is that
  the holder's writes to neighbouring fields invalidate every waiter's copy of the lock line, so the
  spin below re-fetches instead of hitting its own cache. A caller with an array of locks, or with
  one lock beside data written by threads that are not contending for it, wants the padding - and
  gets it by putting the lock in a record that states it, which costs nothing to anyone else.
-}
pub data SpinLock {state: Atomic(Bool)}

-- An unheld lock. Not implicitly zeroed, for `atomic`'s reason: one initialization rule is better
-- than two, and a lock that started out held would be a strange thing to write by omission.
pub fn spinLock() -> SpinLock = SpinLock {state: atomic(False)}

{-
  One attempt, which is one instruction - `xchg` on x86.

  The exchange is the whole of it. Writing `True` unconditionally and reading what was there answers
  "did I take it" in a single indivisible step, where a load followed by a store would let two
  threads both read `False` and both proceed. `compareExchange` would serve as well and costs the
  same instruction here; the exchange asks the simpler question, and has no spurious failure for a
  caller wanting a definite answer to worry about.

  **Acquire on the way in.** Everything the previous holder published before releasing becomes
  visible to this thread once this answers `True`. That edge is the whole point of the primitive, and
  it is attached to the *successful* acquisition: a failed `tryLock` synchronizes with nothing, which
  is right - it observed the lock held and learned nothing about what the holder had done.

  Acquire and not sequential. A total order over this operation and every other sequential one in the
  program is not what mutual exclusion needs: what a lock owes its user is that the critical sections
  do not overlap and that each one sees the last, and that is exactly acquire-release.
-}
pub fn tryLock(self: SpinLock) -> Bool = exchange(self.state, True, UpdateAcquire) == False

{-
  Where the backoff stops doubling - see `lock`.

  Small on purpose. Sixty-four hints is on the order of a few thousand cycles on current x86, which
  is already past the point where a critical section this type is protecting should have finished:
  the cap is not a tuning parameter so much as the place where the answer stopped being "wait longer"
  and became "this is the wrong primitive".
-}
let maxSpinHints = 64 :: Int

{-
  The lock, which is test-and-test-and-**set**.

  The structure is the point, because the obvious loop is substantially worse:

      while exchange(state, True) {}          -- the obvious one, and quadratic under contention

  Every waiter there performs a locked read-modify-write on every turn, and a locked
  read-modify-write takes the cache line **exclusively**. So `n` waiters produce `n` line transfers
  per iteration, each one invalidating the other `n - 1` copies - and the holder, who needs that same
  line to release, is competing with all of them. The traffic grows with the square of the waiters
  and the critical section gets *longer* the more contention there is.

  What is below tries once and, on failure, drops into a loop that only **reads**. A read leaves the
  line shared, so every waiter spins against its own cache and generates no traffic at all until the
  release invalidates them; only then does anyone attempt an exchange again. That is the difference
  between a lock that degrades gracefully and one that collapses.

  `spinHint` is in the read loop for two reasons that are both about the machine rather than the
  algorithm. It stops the processor speculating a long run of loads that a store from another core is
  about to invalidate - which costs a full pipeline flush at the memory-order violation on x86 - and
  it hands the pipeline to the other thread of an SMT core, which may well be the holder.

  ## The backoff, and why it is bounded

  A flat hint loop is a correct lock and a poor citizen: when the line arrives every waiter re-reads
  and re-attempts at once, so a release is followed by a thundering herd of exchanges of which
  exactly one wins. Doubling the hints between reads spreads that out, and costs an uncontended
  caller nothing - it never enters this loop at all, and the first pass through it hints once.

  **There is no fairness.** A thread arriving while the lock is free takes it ahead of one that has
  been spinning, and nothing bounds how often that can happen. That is the ordinary trade for a fast
  uncontended path: a ticket lock or an MCS queue is FIFO and pays an atomic increment and a second
  cache line on every acquisition. If starvation is a real risk here, the critical section is too long
  for this type.
-}
pub fn lock(self: SpinLock) -> {}:
    let &hints = 1

    while !tryLock(self):
        -- Reads only, until the lock looks free. This loop establishes no ordering and `spinHint` is
        -- not a fence: the acquire edge comes from the exchange above that finally wins.
        while self.state.load(LoadRelaxed):
            let &i = 0

            while i < hints:
                spinHint()
                i = i + 1

            if hints < maxSpinHints then hints = hints * 2

{-
  The release, which is one ordinary store.

  A release store and not an exchange, because nothing has to be read: while the lock is held its
  holder is the only thread that may write this word, so there is no race to resolve and no answer to
  want. On x86 that is a plain `mov` - the architecture already orders it behind every write the
  critical section made - which is why a correct unlock costs nothing at all on this machine.

  **Release is what publishes the critical section.** Every write made while holding the lock is
  ordered before this store, and the next acquirer's `tryLock` sees them. Weakening it to relaxed
  would leave a lock that excludes correctly and publishes nothing, which is the kind of defect that
  looks like working code.

  Unlocking a lock this thread does not hold is a program error and is not checked. The check is a
  relaxed load on the release path of every critical section in the program, to catch a mistake a
  `withLock` caller cannot make at all - so `isHeld` is offered below instead, for a test or an
  assertion that decides to pay for it.
-}
pub fn unlock(self: SpinLock) -> {}:
    store(self.state, False, StoreRelease)

{-
  Whether the lock is held, right now, by somebody.

  Relaxed, and therefore **advisory**: the answer is already stale by the time it is returned, and
  using it to decide whether to lock is the check-then-act race the primitive exists to prevent -
  that is `tryLock`, which decides and acts in one step. What this is for is an assertion in a test,
  a debugger's view, and a diagnostic that wants to say what the program's state was.
-}
pub fn isHeld(self: SpinLock) -> Bool = self.state.load(LoadRelaxed)

{-
  The scoped form, and the one almost every caller should write:

      withLock(cell)
      cell.count = cell.count + 1        -- held here, released after the last line of the block

  A **lens**, so the block below the call is the critical section and the release is not a line
  anybody has to remember. What that buys over a higher-order function taking a body is stated in
  doc/spec/lenses.md under "Leaving through a lens": a `return` out of the critical section still
  runs the code after the `yield`, and so does a `break` or a `continue` out of an enclosing loop.
  The continuation reports how it left and the lens's cleanup runs on both paths. Both halves compile
  away where the call is visible, which is every direct call to this.

  Transparent rather than skipping - it yields `{}` and always continues - so the call site needs no
  fallback and reads as a statement.

  What it cannot do is hand a borrow of the protected data back out past the block, and that is
  correct rather than a limitation: a reference outliving the critical section would be a reference
  to data the next holder is free to change.
-}
pub lens fn withLock(self: SpinLock) -> {}:
    lock(self)
    yield {}
    unlock(self)
